<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reşid Mehmed Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1780">1780</a>–<a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reşid Mehmed Pasha was an Ottoman general and grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a>. He restored central authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missolonghi">Missolonghi</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a> and defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-pasha-bushati">Mustafa Pasha Bushati</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> in 1831. In 1836 he led the campaign that forced Mir Muhammad of the Soran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> to surrender. He was defeated by Egyptian forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in 1832.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/resid-mehmed-pasha.jpg" alt="Reşid Mehmed Pasha" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Boggi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_del_pasci%C3%A0_Rescild_-_Giovanni_Boggi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Reşid Mehmed, also known as Kütahı, was born in Circassia and was of Georgian origin. Captured as a child and enslaved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, he entered the service of the Kapudan Pasha Koca Hüsrev Mehmed Pasha, whom his intelligence and ability impressed, and he rose quickly. After a short posting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadorde">Karađorđe</a>'s Serbia he became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kütahya</a> at 29, the origin of his sobriquet. In 1820 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> sent him with other pashas against the rebel Ali Pasha of Yannina, and after Ali's death in 1822 he turned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, who had risen in March 1821.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> he crushed the force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandros-mavrokordatos">Alexandros Mavrokordatos</a> at the Battle of Peta on 4 July 1822, but his first siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missolonghi">Missolonghi</a> with Omer Vrioni failed: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> drew out negotiations until reinforced by sea, the planned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> assault was leaked to them, and the siege was lifted. After subduing Pelion he became commander-in-chief in Rumelia. In April 1825 he invested Missolonghi again with more than 35,000 men and, after quarrelling and then cooperating with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-pasha-of-egypt">Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt</a>, took it when the starving defenders' breakout on 10 April 1826 ended in a massacre.</p><p>Turning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, he besieged the Greek garrison on the Acropolis for ten months, until his victory over a relief force at the Battle of Phaleron on 24 April 1827 forced it to surrender. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1806-1812">Russo-Turkish War</a> he was defeated by General Diebitsch at the Battle of Kulevicha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Reşid Mehmed was grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> from January 1829 to 17 February 1833. He orchestrated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Monastir</a> massacre of 1830, in which hundreds of Albanian beys were killed, and marched against the Bosnian leader Husein Gradaščević, who opposed the grant of autonomy and six Bosnian districts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Gradaščević defeated him at Shtime, but the vizier, aided by Ali-paša Rizvanbegović, later defeated the Bosnian army outside Stup. In the Egyptian-Ottoman War he was captured by Ibrahim Pasha at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in 1832.</p><p>Appointed governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a> eyalets in 1834, he campaigned against the Kurdish tribes and the Yazidi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a>, subdued the Milli tribe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a> in 1835 and defeated the Soran Emirate in 1836. He died the same year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reşid Mehmed Pasha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Egyptian forces under Ibrahim Pasha defeated the Ottomans of <b>Reşid Mehmed Pasha</b> outside Konya, in the war over Muhammad Ali Pasha's demand for control of Greater Syria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1831 the grand vizier <b>Reşid Mehmed Pasha</b> defeated him and forced his surrender at Rozafa castle, restoring direct Ottoman rule over Shkodër.</blockquote><a href="/state/pashalik-of-scutari">Pashalik of Scutari</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1836 an Ottoman army under <b>Reşid Mehmed Pasha</b>, supported by Qajar pressure, forced Mir Muhammad to surrender.</blockquote><a href="/state/soran-emirate">Soran emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Georgia. Died in Diyarbekir eyalet, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.91,40.24&amp;year=1816" aria-label="Diyarbekir eyalet on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5553 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Reşid Mehmed Pasha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6003 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6003 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.91,40.24&amp;year=1816">What was here in 1816? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
